{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PS1 advisory RCA / contract archaeology (2026-08-18):\n\n1. NDJSON close regression — CONFIRMED defect. Import commit 6f701958b7c (2026-06-17) introduced `transition_compute(..., force=True)` as a child-order safety net. The completion close gate was added one day later in 85a77b9cc8 and deliberately keyed its bypass only on `force_close`; therefore importer `force=True` never bypassed completion verification. Commit 0f5dbc88b9 (ticket halfwhite-chordal-stonefly) later revisited this exact call to add mandatory `close_class` while retaining the no-force-close behavior and per-row fail-open. Runtime against pristine PS1 fe4281e07c with the close gate ON and a marker verifier showed the imported task CLOSED, marker calls=0, warnings=[]: the mechanical migration to `force_reason=\"ndjson import safety net\"` accidentally expanded authority. Expected repair: omit force on importer closes; ordinary close-gate policy remains in force and a refused close remains an import warning/open row.\n\n2. Empty-string parity — CONFIRMED incomplete implementation against the approved universal library contract `force: str | None`, where only None means absent and any string is a supplied force reason. Claim CLI archaeology (82186374d7 and current `_parse_force`) already converts bare/`--force=` to `(no reason given)` so bypasses are never audit-empty. Runtime against PS1 showed `claim(force=\"\")` BLOCKED/open while `transition(force=\"\")` BYPASSED/in_progress: `_lib_writes.claim` erased presence via `force or \"\"`, while transition preserved the string and `_force_note` supplied the placeholder. Expected repair: preserve presence on both verbs and normalize an empty supplied reason to `(no reason given)`; do not collapse it to None.\n\n3. Deprecated-alias precedence — CONFIRMED defect. PS1 `_normalize_transition_force` assigns `force_close` last, so the retired alias overwrites the canonical explicit `force`. Runtime with `force=\"canonical\", force_close=\"legacy\"` recorded only `legacy`. The migration contract makes `force_close` a compatibility alias, and the repository-wide explicit-input convention is canonical explicit input wins (e.g. operation snapshot/config override contracts); an alias must fill only when canonical force is absent. Preserve old `force=False, force_close=x` compatibility, while treating every string including empty as an explicit canonical value.\n\n4. Documentation — CONFIRMED stale current guidance. CHANGELOG.md:128 and docs/release-notes.md:288 come from the 0.12 CLI-only rename (63db6ac5a8) and say the library `force_close` kwarg is unchanged. PS1 now deprecates/maps that kwarg to `force`, so newest-first contract notes and the Unreleased changelog must announce the library normalization while the 0.12 historical entry is contextualized rather than left as current advice.\n\nCaller/safety sweep: transition/claim share the command core; automated NDJSON import is the one behavioral dependency on the old split and must remain an ordinary close. MCP transition remains deliberately sequenced in dependent ticket scratchy-leprous-galago; no change here. No new dependency or capability is required. Next: final-form behavioral RED tests for all three executable mechanisms, then focused GREEN + exact-defect mutations and full gates."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787096672459950001,"uuid":"05efde00-f15d-4547-8684-0f053d3ecb43"}